Z||000000||Gold-86SE||04-26-2000||10:51 AM||||Since you guys have been such a great help for me when researching and solving problems, I have another problem to [throw] (corrected by my wife) your way.<P>My wife's 84SE has an OEM stereo. This radio is am/fm/cassette with eq.  We dismantled the [radio housing] (edited by me) to get at the radio and it is out laying on the console.<P>First, before we pulled out the stereo we pulled the fuse specified but the clock stayed on, so I kept pulling fuses until the clock went off. I discovered that the fuse controlling the clock was the fuse that also controlled the interior lights. Question one, is this the correct fuse wiring for the radio in an 84.<P><BR>Other quirks.<P>We have noticed that in the 84 the clock displays the time even when the ignition is turned off, yet my 86SE does not do this.  Is this a correct function of the 84? Or, should it be like the 86 and turn off as the ignition is turned off?<P>It also appears that this OEM radio was pulled out at one time, because the wiring harness(es) going to the back of the radio have been spliced back together.  The colors match correctly, and we have traced the wire colors to the speakers.  It appears that the wiring harness(es) were put back together correctly.<P>Now, the real problem for my wife's radio.  We are not able to turn on the radio, the radio itself appears not to function, yet the clock runs all the time as discussed prior.  <BR>We are not able to turn on the radio power, play cassettes, hear the radio, nothing. What should I be looking for as the cause of the problem.<P>Thanks for the help.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Gold-86SE (edited 04-26-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||04-26-2000||11:11 AM||||Gold, <BR>The second fuse is a "keep alive" source for the clock setting and the radio station presets. It feeds through the small two-wire connector. (Also has the dimmer control from the dash lights.) <BR>As for why the clock stays on. I don't know.<BR>If all the fuses are good, you might try swapping the radios between the two cars. See if the "no radio, constant clock" problem stays with the car, or with the radio. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Foh Fum||04-26-2000||01:58 PM||||Gold,<P>I have an 84 SE myself which was bought new and I have never pulled the radio so it should be wired as factory, right?<P>Here it is:<P>1) When I pull my radio fuse, the clock goes of as well.<P>2) My clock does not display while the ignition is off.<P>3) I too have a problem similar to yours as to the radio powering up although mine does come on a few minutes after hitting the power button.  (better late than never!)  Since it does come on eventually I haven't tackled this as yet.  My hunch, at least in my case, is that it's an internal problem with the radio circuitry and not a power issue.  On that I could be wrong so I'll watch with interest for what you find.  Good luck.<P>I hope this helps.<P>Fum||||reg||1
